    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#francisco-morazan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan.jpg" alt="Francisco Morazán" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Moraz%C3%A1n_Quesada.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aquiles Bigot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Honduras emerged from the dissolution of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a>, the union of the former provinces of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a>, which had become independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> in 1821. In colonial times Honduras was a thinly settled province whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> mines around Tegucigalpa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> ranches supported a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#criollo-people">creole</a> elite, while its governor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishop</a> resided at Comayagua. It was also the homeland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a>, the liberal general who led the federation as its president from 1830 to 1839. As the union disintegrated, the Honduran assembly at Comayagua declared the state’s separation on 26 October 1838, and a constitution of January 1839 established Honduras as a sovereign state. Morazán’s last attempt to restore the federation ended with his execution in Costa Rica in 1842, and conservative generals allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> of Guatemala, notably Francisco Ferrera, the first constitutional president, dominated the following years. Honduras long remained a weak state, invaded repeatedly by Guatemalan, Salvadoran and Nicaraguan forces and pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>, which made the Bay Islands a colony in 1852 and ceded them to Honduras under the Wyke–Cruz Treaty of 1859. The seat of government alternated between Comayagua and Tegucigalpa until 1880, when Tegucigalpa became the permanent capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#marco-aurelio-soto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-aurelio-soto.jpg" alt="Marco Aurelio Soto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcoaureliosoto1894.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julián Moré Cueto, Ministerio de Instrucción Pública de Guatemala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Honduras has been mountainous, rural and poor for most of its history. In the nineteenth century most people were mestizo smallholders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> ranchers in the interior, with Lenca and other indigenous communities in the west, Garífuna and English-speaking Bay Islanders on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, and Miskitu in the eastern Mosquitia. The state had little revenue, and an attempt to build an interoceanic railway with British loans raised in 1867–1870 ended in scandal, leaving a huge foreign debt for less than 100 kilometres of track. From 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a>’s liberal reforms invited foreign mining companies, most famously the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-york">New York</a> and Honduras Rosario Mining Company at San Juancito. The decisive change came with bananas: from the 1890s the Vaccaro brothers, later Standard Fruit, Samuel Zemurray’s Cuyamel Fruit Company and, from 1912, United Fruit acquired vast concessions on the north coast in return for building railways, and by the late 1920s Honduras was the world’s leading banana exporter. The companies built up the ports and towns of La Ceiba, Tela and Puerto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes">Cortés</a> and helped make San Pedro Sula the commercial centre of the country. The great banana strike of 1954 won workers the right to organise. Today Honduras has about ten million people and depends on remittances, garment assembly plants known as <em>maquilas</em>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coffee-economy">coffee</a> and bananas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera.jpg" alt="Rafael Carrera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carrerayturcios_2014-06-22_09-46_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Honduras has seldom been a military power, and for long its armed forces were little more than caudillo followings. In the nineteenth century its territory was a battleground for the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a>: Guatemalan and Salvadoran armies installed or removed Honduran presidents, and at the Battle of La Arada in 1851 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> defeated a combined Honduran and Salvadoran army. The American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#william-walker">William Walker</a>, back in Central America after his expulsion from Nicaragua, was captured by the British and handed to Honduran authorities, who executed him at Trujillo in September 1860. Civil wars between party chiefs continued into the twentieth century, often with foreign backing: in 1911 the former president Manuel Bonilla invaded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a> financed by Samuel Zemurray and regained power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> Marines landed several times between 1903 and 1925, notably during the civil war of 1924. A professional army emerged under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tiburcio-carias-andino">Tiburcio Carías Andino</a> and, after the constitution of 1957 gave it autonomy, became a political force in its own right. In July 1969 Honduras fought the so-called Football War with El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salvador">Salvador</a>, a conflict of about four days over land and migration that killed perhaps 2,000 to 3,000 people. In the 1980s the country hosted US forces at the Palmerola (Soto Cano) air base and camps of the Nicaraguan Contras, while the army’s Battalion 3-16 carried out disappearances of suspected leftists.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tiburcio-carias-andino"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiburcio-carias-andino.jpg" alt="Tiburcio Carías Andino" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tiburcio-carias-andino">Tiburcio Carías Andino</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pres._Andino_of_Honduras_LCCN2016878325.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harris &amp;amp; Ewing, photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of the nineteenth century Honduran governments were short-lived and dependent on caudillos and neighbouring states, and the country adopted more than a dozen constitutions between 1839 and 1982. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a>, president from 1876 to 1883, and his minister Ramón Rosa laid the foundations of the modern state: they reorganised the treasury, schools and legal codes, made Tegucigalpa the capital in 1880 and adopted a liberal constitution that year. Two durable parties emerged, the Liberal Party, founded under Policarpo Bonilla in 1891, and the National Party, which grew out of Manuel Bonilla’s following in the early twentieth century. Foreign fruit companies financed candidates and revolts, and their influence over concessions and congress led many observers to treat Honduras as a company state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tiburcio-carias-andino">Tiburcio Carías Andino</a> of the National Party, elected in 1932, extended his term through constitutional changes and ruled as dictator until 1949, bringing order through repression. Ramón Villeda Morales, president from 1957 to 1963, introduced a labour code, social security and a modest agrarian reform, but was overthrown in October 1963 by Colonel Oswaldo López Arellano, who dominated politics until 1975, when he fell over the ‘Bananagate’ scandal of bribes paid by United Brands. The constitution of 1982, still in force, restored civilian presidential government with a single-chamber National Congress, and the Liberal and National parties alternated in power for the next three decades.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#cold-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cold-war.jpg" alt="Cold War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#cold-war">Cold War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NATO_vs._Warsaw_Pact_(1949-1990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discombobulates, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Honduras’s main place in wider history is as the classic example of a small state shaped by foreign corporations. The American writer O. Henry, who stayed in Trujillo in 1896–1897, coined the phrase ‘banana republic’ in <em>Cabbages and Kings</em> (1904), set in a fictional country modelled largely on Honduras, and the term came to describe any weak state dominated by foreign export interests. The north-coast banana enclaves also shaped regional labour history: the strike of 1954, involving tens of thousands of workers, was one of the largest in Central American history and produced the strongest trade union movement in the region. Honduras’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cold-war">Cold War</a> role as a base for US operations against Sandinista Nicaragua and the Salvadoran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, and later its position on cocaine routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a>, gave it a strategic importance far beyond its size. In culture, the country preserves the Maya city of Copán, inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980 and famous for its sculpted stelae and the Hieroglyphic Stairway, the longest known Maya inscription. The language, dance and music of the Garífuna communities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, shared with Belize, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Nicaragua, were proclaimed a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity by UNESCO in 2001. More recently, the charter cities, or ZEDEs, permitted by a law of 2013 and repealed in 2022, drew international attention.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Honduras remains a presidential republic, but its democratic era since 1982 has been troubled. Civilian presidents took office after the elections of 1981, yet the military remained powerful throughout the 1980s, and only in the 1990s did reforms place the police under civilian authority and end compulsory military service. Hurricane Mitch in October 1998 killed more than 5,000 Hondurans, destroyed much of the country’s infrastructure and set the economy back by years. On 28 June 2009 the army removed President Manuel Zelaya, who had moved closer to Venezuela and was pressing for a poll on a constituent assembly, and flew him to Costa Rica; the coup was condemned internationally and Honduras was suspended from the Organization of American States until 2011. The National Party then held power for twelve years. Juan Orlando Hernández, president from 2014 to 2022, won re-election in 2017 after the Supreme Court set aside the ban on a second term, in a disputed count followed by deadly protests; after leaving office he was extradited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> and convicted of drug trafficking in 2024, then pardoned by Donald Trump in late 2025. Xiomara Castro, Zelaya’s wife, became the first woman president in 2022, and the National Party’s Nasry Asfura narrowly won the disputed election of November 2025. Gang violence, poverty and emigration to the United States remain the country’s central problems.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Honduras is the classic case of a small republic shaped by foreign export companies: the banana concessions on its north coast inspired the phrase ‘banana republic’, and its weak state, military politics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cold-war">Cold War</a> role made it a lasting example of dependence and fragile democracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a>.</em></p>

